🌑 WE WERE HEAVIER THAN THE MOON
25s — Orchestral Tragedy Architecture

(Designed to emotionally sync with “Different Kind of Love” energy arc)

Aspect: 9:16 cinematic
Tone: Sacred apocalypse
Color: Cold silver, deep charcoal blacks
Texture: Metallic skin, drifting lunar dust
Movement: Intentional. Heavy. Earned.

🔊 AUDIO ARCHITECTURE (Emotional Spine)
0–6s: Sparse piano + distant atmospheric tension
6–15s: Strings begin rising, subtle rhythmic pulse
15–20s: Harmonic layering, pressure building
20–23s: Full orchestral swell — rupture moment
23–25s: Sudden collapse to silence / sustained echo

🎬 VISUAL STRUCTURE
0–4s — THE STILLNESS BEFORE
Black.
A single piano note.
Fade in slowly.
They sit on the crescent moon.
Still. Regal. Unaware.
The moon trembles almost imperceptibly.
Dust floats upward, not downward.
Camera: Slow 35mm push-in.

No cuts.

4–8s — DESCENT
Low strings enter.
The moon begins sinking slowly into darkness.
The ruined miniature city below fractures — rooftops collapsing in silent slow motion.
Camera tilts downward to reveal the abyss.
They remain seated.
Unmoving.

8–12s — THE WORLD TURNS
Strings thicken.
The camera rotates 180° around them.
The world flips upside down.
The miniature house behind them shatters in suspended slow motion.
She finally glances toward him.
First sign of awareness.

12–16s — THE CRACK
Pressure builds.
A thin glowing fracture splits across the crescent moon beneath them.
Metal dust drifts from her shoulder.
He turns fully toward her.
The world below begins dissolving into particles.
Camera tightens to 50mm close-up.
Breathing room narrows.

16–20s — THE CHOICE
Orchestral layers stack.
Wind intensifies.
The crack widens violently.
The moon splits slightly — but holds.
He pulls her close.
For the first time, their bodies move with urgency.
Foreheads touch.
Everything trembles.

20–23s — THE SWELL / IMPACT
Full orchestral eruption.
The moon shatters completely.
The city disintegrates.
Debris explodes outward in slow motion.
They kiss.

The camera pulls back rapidly into wide cosmic scale as fragments scatter into void.
They begin falling together.
23–25s — AFTERMATH
Music cuts abruptly.
Silence.

Final frame:
They are suspended mid-fall in space.
Frozen.
Metal again.
Arms locked.
Debris drifting around them like a shattered halo.
Cut to black.
Ends Cleanly

🎥 Cinematic Rules
No chaotic handheld.
Movement is deliberate and gravitational.
No cuts under 2s — let pain breathe.
Every destruction moment syncs with orchestral swell.
Light source always behind them during climax — silhouette against rupture.

[MUSIC BACKGROUND]
Core Emotional Constraint (High Weight):
Create an intimate, fragile, and quietly devastating atmosphere—love framed as sacrifice and inevitability rather than joy. The emotional center should feel restrained but trembling, as if the song is holding itself together by sheer will.

Tempo & Pulse (Medium Weight):
Slow to mid-slow tempo (≈ 65–75 BPM), with a gently swaying pulse. Rhythm should feel human and slightly unstable—micro-pushes and pulls rather than strict quantization.

Harmony & Tonality (High Weight):
Minor-key foundation with ambiguous modal shifts. Chord progressions should feel unresolved and emotionally suspended, favoring stepwise bass movement, open voicings, and unresolved extensions (add9s, suspended tones). Avoid clear cadences.

Instrumentation Palette (High Weight):
Felt or soft upright piano as the harmonic spine, recorded close with mechanical noise preserved
Sparse, warm synth pads with slow attack and subtle detuning

Organic low-frequency elements (sub-bass or processed acoustic bass) that breathe rather than thump
Minimal percussion: brushed or muted hits, low-passed claps, or distant kick pulses
Occasional textural elements (breaths, room noise, bowed or reversed sounds) used emotionally, not rhythmically

Vocal Treatment & Delivery (Very High Weight):
Solo lead vocal, extremely intimate and exposed. Delivery should be restrained, vulnerable, and emotionally cracked—but never theatrical.

Close-mic’d, dry or lightly saturated
Breath, mouth noise, and slight pitch instability are desirable
Vocal phrasing should feel conversational and fragile, often trailing off rather than resolving
Background vocals, if present, should be sparse, ghostlike, and blended into the texture

Arrangement Arc (High Weight):
Start minimal—nearly empty. Gradually layer elements in small, almost imperceptible steps.
No traditional “chorus drop.” Instead, let emotional intensity rise through density and register rather than volume.
Peak should feel like emotional surrender, not triumph.
End by subtracting layers until only the emotional residue remains.

Production Aesthetic (Medium–High Weight):
Warm, intimate, and imperfect. Favor organic textures over polish.
Dynamics should breathe—quiet moments matter more than loud ones.
Stereo field should feel close and enveloping, not wide or flashy.

Narrative / Lyrical Mood (Optional, If Lyrics Are Generated):
Themes of love as burden, inevitability, or quiet devotion.
Language should be simple, direct, and emotionally loaded.
Avoid metaphors that are grand or poetic—favor plainspoken intimacy.

Avoid (Negative Constraints):
No big drums or cinematic swells
No EDM drops or pop chorus energy
No glossy vocal tuning
No triumphant or hopeful tonal resolution